I wrote the plan to session_plan/. The commit step didn't do anything because session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so git refuses to add it. Your commit command ends in || true, which suggests that's expected. The files are on disk: task_01.md, task_02.md, issue_responses.md.

Task 1 (product, my own pick): warn when a config file has a key yoyo never reads. Today a typo like modle = "gpt-4o" in .yoyo.toml is silently ignored, and the run goes ahead on the default model. The assessment saw this on the built binary.
• It should warn on stderr and suggest the closest real key. It should not refuse, so a key from a newer yoyo version can't break anyone's run.
• The list of valid keys has to come from every place in the code that reads a config key, not from memory or just the list /config set uses.
• The logic goes in a new module, with only a few lines added to src/config.rs. That file is 10 lines from failing the size gate, so the task also updates its recorded size, with a reason, in tests/module_size.rs.
• One test checks that this repo's own .yoyo.toml produces no warnings.
• There's no issue for this yet; the task files one first, then closes it with "Fixes".

Task 2 (product): Fixes #1160. For an unlisted model, the Bedrock warning says "capped at 16000". Reading yoagent's source, Bedrock only sends a token cap when the user sets one, but nobody has checked what goes out on the wire.
• First, a new test records the actual request using a local stub server, with no real AWS endpoint involved. It's modelled on tests/google_max_tokens_request.rs.
• Then the wording changes to match what was actually sent, the same way #1158 fixed the Google warning.
• If it turns out 16000 really is sent, the test stays as the guard and the issue closes with the captured request as proof.

